connections from the Raspberry Pi\" and press Apply; allow the firewall on private networks. This PC's address on your network:", ["gh_guide_4"] = "4. Copy the agent to the Pi. In a second terminal on this PC, in the Garden Helper folder:", ["gh_open_folder"] = "Open the folder with the script", ["gh_your_pc"] = "This PC:", ["gh_guide_5"] = "5. In the terminal connected to the Pi, test the connection. It should answer \"Connected\":", ["gh_guide_6"] = "6. Try it with fake sensors. Within seconds the tiles above start to move. Stop with Ctrl+C.", ["gh_guide_7"] = "7. Wire your real sensors (DS18B20 for water, BME280 for air and humidity) and start the agent with --ds18b20 and/or --bme280 instead of --simulate. Relays: vent, fan, water, pump, light (see the full guide).", ["gh_guide_8"] = "8. To start at boot, add --print-service to your command and save its output as /etc/systemd/system/greenhouse-agent.service, then: sudo systemctl enable --now greenhouse-agent. For access from outside the house use a VPN such as Tailscale; never open the port on your router.", ["gh_guide_safety"] = "Safety: wiring mains-powered pumps, valves or heaters must be done by a qualified person with a fuse or RCD. Keep a hardware thermostat as a last line of defence.", ["gh_guide_full"] = "The full guide with wiring diagrams in words and a troubleshooting table is in docs\\RASPBERRY-PI-SETUP.md. The same agent can feed any place, for example soil sensors on a garden bed (pick it as the Sensor source in the garden designer).", ["gh_guide_missing"] = "The script folder was not found next to the app.", ["edit_help"] = "Reshaping a place: drag a corner to move it, click a dot between two corners to add a corner, right-click a corner to remove it. Changes are saved as you go.", ["edit_open_plan"] = "Open its plan", ["edit_done"] = "Done", ["edit_corner_tip"] = "Drag to move. Right-click to remove this corner.", ["edit_add_tip"] = "Click to add a corner here", ["edit_min_corners"] = "A place nee